# Round xmuse3: Muse Spark Open's prompt rate on plan (b′) (`--place bp`: the A6000 the stage, the 3090 an expert tier,
# the host the rest) at the serving defaults, where the common stream rule picks `admit` and the ring is dark. The row
# every Muse stream lever is measured against; no stream lever is in this tree.
# Calibration [measured]: pp512 34.07..34.28 tok/s on plan (a) (A6000 + host, int039 6adfcd6a8, depth-serve 2 rounds,
# ~/.cache/bloomery/muse/box-muse-time.log) = 242 ms a layer a 512-row pass; less the card front ~10 ms and the join
# ~2 ms, the host union over plan (a)'s ~220..225 host experts is ~230 ms: h = 1.02..1.05 ms an expert at m = 16
# columns (XSTREAM_COSTS' t(m) = 28.1 + 57.6 m = 0.95 ms, the band's top, is 7..10 % under it).
# Decomposition per 512-row pass and layer on (b′) [derived]:
#   host CPU   the union over 191 host experts (256 less the stage's ~36 and the tier's ~29) x 1.02..1.05 ms
#              = 195..201 ms: the bound
#   stage SM   front (attention, router, dense) ~10 ms before the union; the stage's ~36 experts as `_sel` slots,
#              16 columns x 18.5 us = 0.30 ms each, ~11 ms, under the union (0 on the wall)
#   tier SM    ~29 experts x 0.30 ms = ~9 ms plus the tier's rows back, under the union (0)
#   PCIe       the rows and ids down, the sums up, ~17 MB: 0.7 ms; the residency's admits ride the pick phase
#   host DRAM  191 x 12.98 MB = 2.5 GB in ~200 ms: 12 GB/s of 137
#   => 207..213 ms a layer x 62 = 12.8..13.2 s a pass: pp512 38.8..40.0. The residency's call picks (`admit`) move
#      the hottest host experts onto the stage's pool for the call and take columns off the union: up to +10 %
#      [derived, no record of Muse's picks]: written 38.5..44.
# Companion (not this card's term): pp4096 the same per pass over eight passes plus the attention's K/V reads
# (plan (a) measured pp4096 = pp512 x 0.997): 38..44.
# The stream levers this row is the base of [derived, docs in the round's report]: the common rule's per-expert test
# (xsplit `streams`, `m_min` 537 > the 512-row pass) keeps the ring dark at 512 on every lane; streaming by the
# balance alone, the ring pass as a route GEMM (card ~0.05 ms an expert), the stage lane under a tier, and a second
# lane on the 3090 take bp's pp512 to ~70, ~93 and ~120..127.
# Sitting (on the lead's go, both cards in the timing witness):
#   BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/xmuse3-pp.card BLOOMERY_TIMING_CARDS=a6000+3090
#     DEPTH_SERVE_CTX=4224' tools/ref/depth-serve.sh <this tree>:512 <this tree>:4096
kind: baseline
question: what does the Muse Spark Open seat's prompt pass run at on plan (b′), the A6000 the stage and the 3090 an
  expert tier beside the host, at the serving defaults (the stream rule's `admit`, the ring dark)?
term: pp tok/s @ P = 512, bloomery-serve --model musespark at the serving defaults (place unset -> bp), A6000 + 3090 +
  host, depth-serve rows (timings.prompt_per_second)
unit: tok/s
predict: 38.5..44
condition: depth-serve under BLOOMERY_TIMING_CARDS=a6000+3090 in its own lease, one fresh server a row; the server's
  `place unset` record names bp, its `plan` record a tier0 card holding experts and its `xstream=` line `admit`, else
  the row is void; the witness (both cards, mem, pgmajfault) shows no other model load and the host's experts
  resident (majflt near 0)
reason: the base row of every Muse stream lever on the two-card default; plan (a)'s row is measured, (b′)'s is not,
  and the levers' predictions are written against it
decide-in: write the row beside plan (a)'s, then open the stream levers in their derived order: the rule's balance
  (common xsplit) and Muse's ring pass as a route GEMM first, then the stage lane under a tier, then the 3090 lane
decide-below: the union is not the only wall: read the tier layers' join wait and the stage's front against the
  union's per-layer time before any stream lever, since a lever on the union predicts less than derived there
decide-above: the call picks move more columns than counted: read the residency's pick records (admits a layer,
  their columns) and re-derive the host columns before pricing the stream levers
